          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "high",
            "summary": "This skill is transparent about building and installing other skills, but its update and uninstall flows have safety gaps that could persist unsafe instructions or delete the wrong files.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "Telegram integration is purpose-aligned, but default discovery loads several generic OpenClaw .env files and imports every key into the process environment, exposing unrelated secrets to later code and subprocesses.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Install is plan-only by default and signed approval is documented, but the approved uninstall flow reads the actual deletion target from a mutable manifest rather than the approved target path, allowing an attacker with manifest write access to redirect deletion within the process user's filesystem permissions.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The evolution flow copies feedback-derived text into a linked reference file and adds feedback-derived trigger cues to SKILL.md. Feedback is redacted for secrets, but instruction-like text, Markdown structure, and role/tool directives are not neutralized before becoming agent-loaded skill material.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill can install generated skills and a macOS LaunchAgent for nightly review. The persistence is disclosed and has plan/uninstall paths, but it compounds the risk of the feedback-to-instructions issue if evolved candidates are approved without careful review.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The stated purpose is coherent with the package: it detects recurring needs, scaffolds candidate skills, validates them, records feedback, proposes updates, and can install or uninstall skills with Telegram approval. These are high-impact agent-behavior mutation capabilities, so the implementation details matter.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              }
            ],
            "guidance": "Use this only in a controlled workspace until the update and uninstall flows are fixed. Prefer plan-only mode, review generated and evolved skill folders before approval, use a dedicated Telegram env file containing only Telegram values, avoid generic .env auto-discovery, and do not enable scheduled or Telegram-approved mutations unless you trust the local install manifest and feedback source.",
            "findings": "[TT3] expected: The Telegram bot token is used in requests to the official Telegram Bot API for approval and reporting. I did not find evidence that unrelated environment variables are intentionally sent to arbitrary network destinations, so this is expected integration behavior rather than confirmed credential exfiltration.\n[TP4] expected: The lower-level install and Telegram wrapper scripts are explicitly listed in SKILL.md and are compatibility wrappers around documented scripts. I did not treat the wrapper pattern itself as hidden tool poisoning.\n[T01] unexpected: The finding is supported: propose_skill_update.py writes raw feedback fields into references/evolution-feedback.md and links that file from SKILL.md, while validation focuses on structure and does not neutralize instruction injection.\n[T09] unexpected: The uninstall finding is supported: approval checks target_root/skill_name, but uninstall_skill() later deletes record['target'] from .skill-forge-installs.json without containment or equality checks against the approved path.\n[T09] unexpected: The environment-loading finding is supported: telegram_config.py loads multiple generic env files and copies all keys into os.environ even though the Telegram workflow only needs bot token and chat ID.",
            "agenticRiskFindings": null,
            "riskSummary": null,
